# Copy to deploy/.env (git-ignored). Compose auto-loads .env from the
# project directory, uses it for ${...} interpolation in compose.yaml, and
# (since Story 1.5) passes the whole file into the api container via
# `env_file:` — so every field on `Settings` is settable here, not only the
# ones interpolated below. Before that, keys the compose file did not
# mention were read and silently discarded.
#
# The e2e stack (compose.e2e.yaml) deliberately does *not* read this file:
# its specs assert against the default thresholds, and a local override
# leaking into them would fail the suite with no defect behind it.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
# Rotatable at any time: the api reconciles the lineworker_app role with this
# value on every boot, so changing it after the DB volume exists is fine.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD is not — Postgres only reads it when initializing an empty
# volume, so changing that one needs an ALTER ROLE or `down -v`.
APP_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-too
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Session auth (Story 1.3). The cookie carries an opaque session id only —
# role and employer scope are re-resolved from the database per request, so
# shortening the TTL costs a re-login and nothing else.
# SESSION_TTL_HOURS=12
# Secure flag defaults to on when ENV=prod and off otherwise (dev and e2e
# stacks are plain http, where a Secure cookie would never be sent). Set it
# explicitly only when terminating TLS in front of a non-prod stack.
# SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true

# Derived-value parameters are NOT env knobs (Story 2.1). The severity band
# the High Risk tile counts moved into the versioned `derivation_thresholds`
# rule document when ZEN landed (AD-8), so RISK_HIGH_MIN and RISK_MED_MIN no
# longer exist as settings — setting them here does nothing. Changing a band
# means seeding a new version of that document in an Alembic migration,
# which is deliberate: the number that decides what counts as a high-risk
# injury should arrive with a reviewed diff and an effective date, not with
# an operator's restart.

# SLA targets (Story 1.5). The strip reports pass/warn against these, and
# the comparison is strict on both sides: a book averaging exactly 1.0d
# pick misses a 1.0 target, and exactly 80% RTW misses an 80 target.
# Both moves are AD-8 parameters — until ZEN JDM owns them, this is where
# they live.
# SLA_PICK_TARGET_DAYS=1
# SLA_APPROVE_TARGET_DAYS=5
# SLA_SETTLE_TARGET_DAYS=30
# SLA_RTW_TARGET_PCT=80

# The payment batch (Story 3.4). Approved payments are disbursed when the
# batch runs; these two knobs decide when that is, and they are different
# questions — the cadence is *which days money goes out*, the tick is only how
# often the process checks whether one of those days has arrived. Conflating
# them is how a twice-weekly job ends up running every five minutes.
#
# Day names rather than numbers, because this is a value an operator reads:
# `[1,4]` is a value nobody can check at a glance. An unparseable day stops
# the process at startup naming what was typed, rather than leaving a batch
# that silently never runs.
# PAYMENT_BATCH_WEEKDAYS=tue,fri
# SCHEDULER_TICK_SECONDS=300
#
# The scheduler is on by default and off under ENV=e2e, where the suite
# triggers the batch explicitly so its figures cannot move because a Tuesday
# arrived mid-run. Set this to watch the real thing run in a local e2e stack,
# or to hold a dev stack still. The batch remains idempotent either way: a run
# with nothing approved pays nothing and writes no audit rows.
# SCHEDULER_ENABLED=false

# --- Local model serving and embeddings (Story 6.1) ---------------------
#
# All inference is local. The `ollama` service in compose.yaml declares **no
# published port** in any profile — it is reachable from the api container on
# the internal network and from nowhere else — so there is no URL here to point
# at a hosted provider, and no code path in the build that could use one if
# there were (AD-5).
#
# **First boot downloads several gigabytes.** The ollama container serves,
# pulls both models below, and only then reports healthy; `api` waits for that,
# so the first `docker compose up` on a clean machine is as slow as your
# connection. The models live in the `ollama-models` volume, so every boot after
# that is instant and only `docker compose down -v` re-downloads them. The
# healthcheck's start_period is thirty minutes for exactly this.
#
# Model names are configuration rather than literals in code, because the exact
# `qwen3` tag is an open architecture decision pending a re-benchmark. Sizing
# from the architecture's model table: qwen3:14b wants ~24 GB of GPU, qwen3:32b
# ~48 GB. On a machine with no GPU, qwen3:8b is the documented fallback and is
# slow but correct — nothing in Story 6.1 calls the chat model at all.
# **Story 6.2 is the first caller.** Insight generation issues up to four chat
# completions per claim through `agents/client.py`, and the name below is what
# `ai_insight.model` records and what each card is labelled with — so on a
# laptop this is still the knob to turn down, but it now buys something.
# qwen3:8b is the documented CPU pairing: slow, and correct.
# CHAT_MODEL=qwen3:14b
#
# **Changing EMBEDDING_MODEL to a model of a different dimensionality is a
# migration, not a config flip.** bge-m3 emits 1024 floats and the
# claim_embedding / knowledge_embedding columns are vector(1024). The
# architecture's constrained-hardware pairing names nomic-embed-text, which
# emits 768: point this at it against the current schema and the first refresh
# run fails loudly with EmbeddingDimensionMismatch naming the model, the length
# it returned and the length the column wants. That is deliberate — padding or
# truncating would fill the table with vectors that retrieve nonsense and
# nothing on any screen would explain it. Moving to a 768-dimension model means
# an Alembic revision that alters both columns and a full re-embed.
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=bge-m3
#
# The embedding refresh job. Two knobs and they answer different questions: the
# interval is how long a stale embedding may stay stale, while
# SCHEDULER_TICK_SECONDS above is only how often the process checks whether any
# job is due. The batch size bounds one run — pending work is not lost, it is
# still pending on the next tick, which is what makes bounding it safe here and
# would not make it safe for a payment run.
# EMBEDDING_REFRESH_INTERVAL_SECONDS=900
# EMBEDDING_REFRESH_BATCH_SIZE=25
#
# Per-request timeout on the embeddings call. Generous because a cold model on
# a CPU-only box takes tens of seconds to answer its first request, and the
# alternative is a refresh that can never succeed on hardware the architecture
# explicitly permits for dev.
# EMBEDDING_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
#
# --- The AI insight cache (Story 6.2) ---------------------------------------
#
# **LANGSMITH_TRACING / LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2 / LANGSMITH_OTEL_ENABLED cannot be
# turned on, and setting them here does nothing.** The chat client's vendor
# stack (`langchain-ollama` → `langchain-core`) hard-depends on `langsmith`,
# whose tracer activates from those variables alone and POSTs the entire prompt
# and the entire completion to api.smith.langchain.com. That is a claim's
# clinical narrative leaving the network (AD-11) through a code path nobody in
# this repository wrote, and AD-5 says there is no cloud path at all — so
# `config.py` overwrites all of them with `false` at import and every compose
# profile repeats it in `environment:`, which outranks this file. There is no
# supported way to enable it; a story that wanted tracing would be proposing a
# cloud code path.
#
# The insight refresh job, and the two numbers are deliberately unlike the
# embedding refresh's above. An embedding tick is one batched request over short
# summaries; an insight tick is **four chat completions per claim** against a
# server that answers one request at a time — so the interval is longer and the
# batch is much smaller, and the batch counts *claims* rather than rows.
#
# The job's job is to finish the first pass: a claim is pending while it is
# missing one of its four narratives, and once all four exist it drops out. An
# insight is a cache rendered with its own generation timestamp (AD-10), so an
# hour-old narrative is a dated answer rather than a wrong one, and
# re-generating the portfolio on a timer would spend completions for ever to
# replace text that says the same thing. Regeneration is on demand: the tab's
# Refresh button, through the same command.
# INSIGHT_REFRESH_INTERVAL_SECONDS=3600
# INSIGHT_REFRESH_BATCH_SIZE=5
#
# Per-request timeout on a chat completion. Twice the embeddings timeout because
# generation is a different order of work — an embedding is one forward pass
# over a short summary, a structured narrative is tens of tokens decoded one at
# a time — and the architecture explicitly permits a CPU-only dev box.
# CHAT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120
#
# How old a comparable claim's vector may be before the similar-case card says
# so. AD-12 requires retrieval to carry `embedded_at` and answers to disclose
# staleness past a configured threshold; this is that threshold. Seven days is
# long enough that an ordinary fifteen-minute refresh cadence never trips it and
# short enough that a refresh job which has been failing for a week is visible
# on the card rather than only in a log.
# INSIGHT_STALENESS_DISCLOSURE_DAYS=7
#
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL defaults to http://ollama:11434 — the compose service name.
# Override it only to point a host-run api process at a compose stack's model
# server, which needs a published port that this project deliberately does not
# provide; the supported way to develop against the model is to run the stack.
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434

# --- The copilot's honest degradation (Story 6.6) ---------------------------
#
# What the console does when the local model server stops answering. These are
# the four knobs `config.py` argues at length that a deployment should tune, and
# a deployment reads *this* file — so they are named here rather than left to be
# discovered in the source.
#
# How many times one model call is attempted before the run tells the handler
# the model is unavailable. Only a call that failed **before its first streamed
# token** is retried: the copilot streams, so a second attempt after tokens have
# reached the transcript would put the answer on screen twice.
#
# **Raising this is arithmetic, not taste, and the process checks it.** Against
# a server that accepts the connection and then hangs, the retry doubles the
# worst case rather than shortening it: two attempts at the 120 s
# CHAT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS plus one backoff is 240.5 s inside the 300 s
# COPILOT_RUN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS. Three attempts do not fit, and a run the wall
# clock cut off is reported as `ai_limit` — "a bound this deployment set" —
# where the truth was an outage. The api refuses to start on a combination that
# cannot fit, naming both numbers, rather than mislabelling every outage.
# CHAT_MAX_ATTEMPTS=2
#
# The wait between those attempts. Long enough that a container mid-restart has
# moved on, short enough that a handler facing a genuinely dead model server is
# told so rather than watching a spinner. Zero is legal and means "retry
# immediately".
# CHAT_RETRY_BACKOFF_SECONDS=0.5
#
# The availability probe — `GET /api/version` on OLLAMA_BASE_URL, the same path
# the model container's own healthcheck polls. It exists to drive the copilot
# panel's disabled state, and **nothing on a run path consults it**: a run
# attempts the model and reports what actually happened, because two sources of
# truth about reachability would eventually disagree.
#
# Deliberately far shorter than CHAT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS: the question is
# whether anything is answering on the model port, not whether it can decode a
# thousand tokens, and a probe that took two minutes to report an outage would
# report it long after the handler had found out another way.
# AI_HEALTH_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3
#
# How long one probe result is reused. This is what makes the endpoint cheap
# enough for every open panel to poll: N clients on a ten-second cache are one
# upstream request per ten seconds regardless of N. It is a staleness bound and
# not a correctness one — the worst a stale answer costs is one run that fails
# honestly, or a few seconds of a greyed-out composer after a recovery, and the
# panel's "Try again" control bypasses the cache outright so a handler never
# waits it out.
# AI_HEALTH_PROBE_CACHE_SECONDS=10

# --- PHI purge cascade & retention (Story 8.1) ------------------------------
#
# Two retention floors and two cadences, and the floors are the numbers that
# matter. AD-11 classes everything claim-derived as PHI — checkpoints,
# embeddings, the insight cache, audit diffs and binaries alike — and gives the
# audit log and the copilot's conversations *different* obligations, which is
# why these are two knobs and not one.
#
# **The audit floor is a minimum, and lowering it here is not enough.** It says
# how long an audit row must be KEPT: the row is the evidence that somebody did
# something, so destroying it early is the failure the number exists to prevent.
# It is enforced in two places on purpose. The housekeeping job reads this
# variable, and the `audit_redactor_delete` row-level-security policy carries
# the floor baked in by migration 0003 — deliberately not driven from a session
# setting, because a policy the guarded session can set is not a safety net (see
# `data/versions/20260822_0049_purge_grants.py`). So **changing this value also
# requires a migration** that moves the policy with it. If the two disagree the
# job does not under-delete quietly: it counts the rows past its own cutoff,
# compares that against what the policy allowed it to delete, logs
# `retention.floor_mismatch` and raises.
# AUDIT_RETENTION_YEARS=7
#
# **The checkpoint floor is a maximum, and is the opposite kind of number.** A
# copilot checkpoint is a transcript quoting an injured worker's diagnosis, wage
# and fraud indicators back verbatim, so ninety days is a ceiling on exposure —
# long enough that a handler returning to a claim after a quarter still has the
# conversation, short enough that a thread is not an indefinite second copy of
# the case file. It is enforced by code alone; no policy backs it up, because
# `copilot_thread` is an ordinary table this project owns.
#
# It bounds a conversation's AGE rather than its idleness: the checkpoint tables
# carry no timestamp this codebase may read (they are the saver's, under AD-3's
# vendored-migration exception), so the sweep keys on `copilot_thread.created_at`
# and a thread created ninety-one days ago is purged even if it was used
# yesterday.
# COPILOT_CHECKPOINT_RETENTION_DAYS=90
#
# How often each sweep runs. Cadences, not floors — the same two-knob split
# PAYMENT_BATCH_WEEKDAYS/SCHEDULER_TICK_SECONDS keeps above, and conflating them
# is how a seven-year floor ends up being swept every five minutes. Daily for
# both, because a cutoff that moves by one day per day gains nothing from firing
# more often. Both jobs are idempotent: a run with nothing past its floor
# deletes nothing, writes no audit row and logs nothing at all, which is what
# keeps a daily job from burying the runs that did something.
# AUDIT_RETENTION_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400
# CHECKPOINT_RETENTION_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400
#
# There is no knob here for the purge cascade itself, and that is deliberate: it
# has no HTTP endpoint and no UI (admin exposure rides the deferred IdP
# decision). Purging one subject is a management command run by somebody with a
# shell on the host —
#
#     docker compose -f deploy/compose.yaml exec -T api \
#         uv run --no-dev python -m scripts.purge_claim WC-20017
#
# — and it relies on ALEMBIC_DATABASE_URL, which both compose profiles already
# set on the api service, because the redaction half runs `SET ROLE
# audit_redactor` on that owner connection. The application role cannot redact:
# its audit_event grants are INSERT and SELECT by design, and that is the
# invariant the whole audit design rests on. A deployment that clears the
# variable gets a refusal naming the role before anything is deleted, never a
# half-purge.

# --- TLS at ingress and to Postgres (Story 8.2) -----------------------------
#
# Two host directories, and they are the only knobs in this file with no
# default: the prod overlay guards both with `${…:?}`, so
# `docker compose -f deploy/compose.yaml -f deploy/compose.gpu.yaml -f
# deploy/compose.prod.yaml config` fails with a message naming the variable
# rather than mounting an empty anonymous volume and failing at start with a
# missing-file error three layers down. Neither is read by any other profile:
# Story 1.1's plain-HTTP decision stands for dev, and the e2e suite drives
# http://localhost:8081.
#
# Both point at the host secret store. **Nothing cryptographic belongs in
# version control** — not the key, not the certificate, not the CA — which is
# also why CI can render the prod profile and cannot boot it, and why the two
# commands at the bottom of this section exist.
#
# **These two are written uncommented, unlike every optional knob in this
# file.** Everything else here is a default this build already has, commented
# out so that copying the template changes nothing; these have no default and
# the overlay refuses to render without them. Left commented they would be the
# only two settings in a copied file that are inert *and* mandatory, which is a
# `docker compose config` failure at the end of a deployment rather than a value
# somebody edited at the start. Replace the paths; do not delete the lines.
#
# TLS_CERT_DIR is mounted read-only at /etc/nginx/tls and must contain
# server.crt and server.key for the deployment's hostname — the name browsers
# ask for.
TLS_CERT_DIR=/etc/lineworker/tls
#
# PG_TLS_DIR must contain three files: server.crt and server.key for the
# database server, and ca.crt — the authority that signed server.crt — which is
# what the api verifies the chain against. Postgres refuses to start if the key
# is group- or world-readable and not owned by the server's user, so
# `chmod 0600` and `chown 999` on the host before the first `up`.
#
# The two services get different mounts, deliberately: postgres gets the
# directory (it needs the key), and api gets `${PG_TLS_DIR}/ca.crt` mounted as
# the single file /etc/postgresql/tls/ca.crt. The api verifies a chain and has
# no use for the database's private key; handing it to the container that
# parses handler input and model output would let a foothold there impersonate
# the database to the application, which is worse than the exposure
# `verify-full` was added to close.
#
# **The Postgres certificate is issued for the name `postgres`, not for the
# deployment's hostname, and this is the knob's one real trap.** `verify-full`
# checks the certificate against the host the *URL* names, and both prod URLs
# connect to `postgres` — the compose service name on the internal network. So
# server.crt's subjectAltName must contain `postgres`; a certificate carrying
# the public hostname fails the check at the api's first connection, with the
# api up and the database unreachable. TLS_CERT_DIR's certificate and this one
# are therefore **two different certificates** with two different names. For a
# self-signed pair, `openssl req -x509 -addext "subjectAltName=DNS:postgres"`
# is the part that is easy to leave out.
PG_TLS_DIR=/etc/lineworker/pg-tls
#
# **`sslmode=verify-full`, not `require`.** `require` encrypts the connection
# and verifies nothing about the other end, so it defeats a passive listener
# and not an impostor that has joined the compose network; `verify-full` checks
# the chain against ca.crt *and* that the certificate's name matches the host
# the URL asked for. Both prod URLs carry it. Nothing else in the URL changes:
# the app role still connects as lineworker_app and Alembic still as the owner.
#
# **The CA path is an environment variable rather than a URL parameter, and
# that is a driver fact rather than a preference.** libpq takes it as
# `sslrootcert=` in the connection string, and psycopg would be happy with
# that — but every async path in this build speaks asyncpg, whose `connect()`
# has no `sslrootcert` keyword at all and reads the file parameters only from a
# DSN or from PGSSLROOTCERT (`asyncpg/connect_utils.py`). SQLAlchemy's asyncpg
# dialect passes a URL's query parameters straight through as keyword
# arguments, so `?sslrootcert=…` would raise on the first connection, in prod,
# and nowhere else. PGSSLROOTCERT is honoured by both drivers, so the prod
# overlay sets that and `Settings` refuses the file parameters in a URL
# outright, naming this variable in the error. `sslmode` itself needs no such
# care: `config.py::_with_driver` renames it to `ssl` for the asyncpg engines,
# which accept the identical libpq strings under that name.
#
# ## Verifying it, on a real deployment
#
# Neither check can run in CI — there are no certificates in version control to
# run it against — so `server/tests/test_deploy_tls_posture.py` asserts the
# configuration and these two commands assert the running system. Both are part
# of the deployment checklist rather than optional curiosity.
#
# At the ingress, from anywhere that can reach the host:
#
#     openssl s_client -connect <host>:443 -servername <host> </dev/null
#
# — expect the certificate chain and `Verify return code: 0 (ok)`. A plain
# `curl -I http://<host>/` should answer `308` with an `https://` Location.
#
# For the API's own connection the question is whether *those* sessions are
# encrypted, not whether the server would accept an encrypted one from
# somewhere else — so it is asked of the server, about the pooled connections
# the api is holding right now, while the stack is up and has served a request:
#
#     docker compose -f deploy/compose.yaml -f deploy/compose.gpu.yaml \
#         -f deploy/compose.prod.yaml exec -T postgres \
#         psql -U lineworker -d lineworker -c \
#         "SELECT usename, ssl, version FROM pg_stat_ssl JOIN pg_stat_activity \
#          USING (pid) WHERE usename IN ('lineworker_app','lineworker')"
#
# Every `lineworker_app` row must read `t` with a TLS version of 1.2 or 1.3. An
# `f` means that connection fell back to plaintext and DATABASE_URL is not what
# you think it is. Asking the server rather than opening a fresh connection is
# what makes this a check on the *running api* — a new session started for the
# purpose could be encrypted while the pool's are not.
#
# The single-session form the same question takes from any client, for
# reference and for a quick check from psql itself:
#
#     SELECT ssl, version FROM pg_stat_ssl WHERE pid = pg_backend_pid();
#
# Note that these prove the asyncpg path too, which is the one the URL
# translation above exists for: the api's pooled connections are the ones
# `config.py::_with_driver` renamed `sslmode` to `ssl` for, and a failure of
# that translation would show up here as no `lineworker_app` rows at all —
# the api would not have started.
